Thanks for all the suggestions/ideas.  I have been working on this off and =
on this week, and here's an outline of what I've written:

[Client-side]
modded Vulture launch script (shell script):

1) Prior to launching vulture, run upload-scores to get any unsynced scores=
 found in outgoing/scores dir, in case there were connection issues.
2) rsync server record file to overwrite client's Vulture record file
3) Record last modified time of record file
4) vulture runs
5) check if client record file was modified by Vulture, run extract-scores =
if so

extract-scores script (shell script):

1) get timestamp, and username
2) tail -n 1 logfile >> outgoing/scores/<username><timestamp>.score (last l=
ine of logfile will be what was most-recently added to the record file)
3) run upload-scores script

upload-scores script (shell script):

1) rsync all score files in outgoing/scores to server incoming/scores
2) server-side rsync daemon runs nethack-merge-scores as a post-xfer exec
3) clear all files in outgoing/scores if successful

[Server-side]
nethack-merge-scores script (Ruby script):
For each score file in incoming/scores:
1) Read score line from score file, parse out score, close
2) Exclusively lock master record file.  Blocks until we can acquire a lock=
.
3) Parse score value of each score line in record, compare against incoming=
 score.
4) When we find record score < incoming score, remember line number
5) Put incoming score line into list to write
6) Read remaining lines of record into write list, don't parse (no need sin=
ce we found insert position)
7) Rewind file, set line number to insert position
8) Write each line of write list to file
9) Close file, unlock

It seems to be working quite well!

Some limitations:
1) I assume Vulture won't be in use by multiple people concurrently on the =
same machine. =20
Who knows what people are trying out there, but I'm guessing a majority of =
Vulture users don't do this.  Accommodating, with the current setup, would =
require knowing the player names of each user, then parsing out the most re=
cent score by that player name in the logfile.
2) Similarly, I assume the player is not playing multiple, concurrent games=
 with the same player name on different systems. =20
If this did happen, not a big deal, but the player would be able to get aro=
und the restrictions Jukka mentioned.  Would just be some extra checks in t=
he merge script to correct for this though.
3) Connection issues updating the record prior to starting the game might a=
lso allow the score restrictions to be bypassed.
4) I assume people won't try to cheat. =20
There's nothing in place right now that differentiates between a score writ=
ten by Vulture, and a score made up by a cheater with a text editor.  If I =
were to expand this into a general solution (I do want to eventually), I'd =
have to address this, but for now I'm content to make participants feel dee=
p guilt in their hearts about cheating.

A couple comments:
1) As far as I can tell, I don't need to actually merge the entire client-s=
ide record file back into the master record, just what's new.
2) While many scores could be added on the server between when the player u=
pdates their record file, and then finishes their Vulture session, the main=
 point of having a synced score file is to let Vulture determine if the sco=
re really needs added for the current player, using the compiled-in score r=
estrictions (this does allow the issue mentioned in limitation 2 though).
3) Everywhere I am doing an rsync, I'm actually calling a script which sets=
 up an SSH tunnel first, for exactly the reason mentioned by Jurgen (LoL @ =
Cheap.V.i.a.g.r.a. killed by a succubus, while helpless).  Don't want to ex=
pose a public, rsync-writable location.
4) There are some sanity checks in the merge script not listed, like aborti=
ng on an invalid incoming score, aborting on a duplicate score entry, and a=
ccounting for inserting into an empty record file.
5) No reason the server-side script had to be in Ruby, other than my enjoyi=
ng the language.  Didn't want to use it on the client though since I don't =
want to assume the presence of a Ruby interpreter.
6) In the future, a better course of action might be to patch the Vulture s=
ource and have it grab scores just prior to attempting to add a new entry, =
then immediately sending the new entry to the server if it determines it sh=
ould add one.  Could better address the listed limitations, and would make =
a lot of the stuff done in those client-side scripts unnecessary.
